---

<div class="prose">
  <slot />
</div>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-inline: auto;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .prose > :global(* + *) {
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
  }

  /* Direct-child combinator, not a plain descendant selector — real bug,
     not a style choice: the mid-article subscribe box is raw HTML
     injected directly into the markdown AST (remark-inline-subscribe.mjs)
     as a sibling of the article's own paragraphs/headings, so its own
     internal <h2> ("Get one good coffee idea a week.") sits one level
     deeper in the DOM (inside .email-capture) than an authored markdown
     heading does (a direct child of .prose itself) — confirmed directly
     via the real DOM, not assumed. A plain `.prose :global(h2)`
     descendant selector matched BOTH, giving the card's own heading an
     unwanted 64px margin-top on top of the card's own padding — visibly
     top-heavy, more noticeable once the surface colors got a real
     distinguishable dark-mode palette, but present in both themes; it was
     never actually theme-specific. `.email-capture h2`'s own rule (no
     margin-top, by design — the card's padding already provides the gap)
     had equal CSS specificity to this one, so whichever stylesheet
     happened to load first silently won — exactly the kind of
     source-order fragility already flagged and fixed once this session
     for the Reflection pull-quote. Scoping to direct children here is the
     structural fix: it only ever matches the real authored headings,
     never a nested card's own heading, regardless of cascade order. */
  .prose > :global(h2) {
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
  }

  .prose > :global(h3) {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }

  .prose :global(img) {
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  .prose :global(a) {
    text-underline-offset: 0.15em;
  }

  .prose :global(blockquote) {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--copper);
    padding-left: var(--space-5);
    color: var(--espresso);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .prose :global(ul),
  .prose :global(ol) {
    padding-left: var(--space-6);
  }

  .prose :global(code) {
    background: var(--paper);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 0.15em 0.4em;
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }

  .prose :global(pre) {
    background: var(--paper);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: var(--space-4);
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .prose :global(pre code) {
    background: none;
    padding: 0;
  }
</style>
